WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.117 --> 00:00:02.017
Hey! Hey! Look at me!

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.602
Now, the one thing I
hate when they do movies

00:00:05.602 --> 00:00:08.060
is every time you get some
sort of torso injuries,

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.470
they make them spit blood,
and that doesn't happen.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.315
You don't vomit blood
from getting a gut shot

00:00:12.315 --> 00:00:13.290
or anything like that.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.890
Hi, my name is Peter Rhee.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:18.270
I served in the military for 24 years,

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.438
and I'm a trauma surgeon.

00:00:19.438 --> 00:00:21.970
I was one of the first
surgeons into Afghanistan.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.660
Today we're going to be
looking at battle-wound scenes

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.803
in movies and judging how real they are.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.240
This is a really good scene.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.590
When that explosion
went off in slow motion,

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.630
you could see the boot
flying right at the camera.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.470
It's a little bit unusual
from an injury point of view.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.450
A shell coming in hitting the ground

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.480
would take his legs off like that.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.720
Because seeing bilateral amputees

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.600
at the thigh has been
a really recent injury

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.900
that we never saw before in previous wars.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.489
We didn't really see bilateral
amputations above the knee.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:58.670
Before, in the other war movies,

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.690
they would step on
Claymores and landmines.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.090
And those were actually meant

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.170
to take off your foot and your ankle,

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:06.910
so the medics would come out,

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.510
and then you'd sniper the medics

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:09.653
and get more casualties.

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:12.290
So it wasn't really supposed
to be a mortal wound.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.550
His was mangled below
the knee, but obviously,

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.930
his was going to be close to
what we were seeing in Iraq,

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:22.050
where they would just
build these huge bombs

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.630
and bury them into the ground.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:27.003
We were seeing just legs blown sky-high.

00:01:30.611 --> 00:01:32.990
I could do three hours on tourniquets.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.780
Blood loss is the No. 1 preventable loss

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.046
that we want to avoid.
Some of the soldiers,

00:01:38.046 --> 00:01:40.440
they even wear tourniquets
when they're going out there.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.140
Actually, what we are
recommending and what we teach

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.090
is that they put on your own tourniquets.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.940
The tourniquets are problematic

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.510
because if you don't put it on right

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.030
and if it's not on absolutely
the tightest it can be,

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.030
you'll bleed more and you'll bleed out.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.046
When you put it on, you
got to commit to it.

00:01:58.046 --> 00:01:59.480
You got to go full bore.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.571
And I tell you, putting a tourniquet on

00:02:01.571 --> 00:02:03.530
will be the most painful
thing you experienced

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:04.680
about three to four minutes.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.320
It's like your leg is falling off.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.370
So it is something you
do as a last resort.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.352
It will save lives, but
you got to go for it.

00:02:12.352 --> 00:02:14.260
Otherwise it will actually bleed more.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.093
Desmond: Here comes the morphine,

00:02:16.093 --> 00:02:17.616
here comes the morphine.

00:02:17.616 --> 00:02:19.010
It's gonna kick in real quick, Ralph.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.460
We're gonna get you shifted.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.660
In the old days, morphine was good.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.307
We got scared of it

00:02:24.307 --> 00:02:26.160
because morphine can definitely kill you.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.825
If you were to take a person
who's low blood pressure,

00:02:28.825 --> 00:02:29.920
not a lot of blood circulating,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.020
and then you give it,

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.773
then it can depress your cardiac function,

00:02:33.773 --> 00:02:34.690
and then you can die.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:35.930
You would also stop breathing.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.410
So we've actually gone away
from morphine in the field.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.120
We use a new drug called ketamine.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.910
While it provides a lot of relief of pain,

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:44.610
it does not lower your blood pressure.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.080
So that's the reason why we use it.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.600
The medics can do a lot, and
they're so vitally important,

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.088
but a lot of the movies,

00:02:52.088 --> 00:02:54.174
I think the audience doesn't understand

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:55.940
that a medic is not a trauma surgeon.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.470
Trauma surgeon obviously
went to medical school,

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.500
did five years of general surgery,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.300
then two years of training
as a trauma surgeon.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.420
So seven years of training,
like a brain surgeon.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.610
The medic's job is to get them to us.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.480
Not to clamp things in the field.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.500
It's pretty good.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.833
I'd say give it a 9.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.560
Now we know that IV
bags actually hurt you.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.690
We no longer use crystalloid
solution in trauma,

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.400
even when your blood pressure falls.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.853
The studies that we're shown is that

00:03:23.853 --> 00:03:26.483
the people who don't get those IV bags,

00:03:26.483 --> 00:03:28.720
they have a better chance of surviving.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.610
If you give them that fluid,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:31.750
their blood pressure goes up,

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:32.690
and then you bleed more.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.031
Back then, that was
the only way we did it.

00:03:35.031 --> 00:03:37.763
We are only finally recognizing that

00:03:37.763 --> 00:03:40.766
saltwater solution doesn't
resemble blood at all.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.995
I think he put a mosquito
clamp in the mouth to do it.

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:50.540
That was turning me off a little bit.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.413
I could see it

00:03:51.413 --> 00:03:53.760
doing it with bare hands is a possibility.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.440
We used to do a lot of this in bare hands.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.630
And when I started my residency,

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.320
we were doing it bare hands too.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.940
So that's not too unrealistic.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.640
That area is called a junctional bleed,

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.210
where you can't put a tourniquet on it.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.460
So we're still trying to
invent ways to stop bleeding

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.050
when you get shot at the
groin or in the armpit,

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.962
because you can't put a
tourniquet into your armpit.

00:04:11.962 --> 00:04:12.989
I got it.

00:04:12.989 --> 00:04:14.546
It's going back in!

00:04:14.546 --> 00:04:15.554
Hold it! Hold it!

00:04:16.429 --> 00:04:18.150
This was not a good scene for me.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.440
I've used it for training lots of times.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:21.650
The way they did the artery,

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.010
clamping the artery, and so on.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.610
For surgeons and people
who do vascular surgery,

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:26.760
it wasn't very realistic.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.150
It is somewhat chaotic.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.660
Obviously somebody had
a flashlight on there.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.410
That's the reason why we always
wear headlights on our head.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:36.520
For trauma out there, you don't need much.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:38.260
You just need some basic retractors.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:39.770
You got to have good lighting.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.940
And then the second thing
is you got to have suction.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.667
If you don't have suction nowadays,

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:46.458
it's really tough just
working with sponges

00:04:46.458 --> 00:04:47.958
and ways to soak up stuff.

00:04:47.958 --> 00:04:50.385
For what I do, you don't
need a lot of stuff.

00:04:50.385 --> 00:04:52.550
So that is a major problem.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.200
The wounds were OK.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.497
I'd give this about a 6.

00:04:56.497 --> 00:04:58.263
Expectant. Routine.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.043
Priority.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:02.590
He's gone.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:03.820
It's a great movie.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.700
It's pretty well done.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.048
Triage in the field is very different

00:05:08.048 --> 00:05:10.320
than triage when you come
to a medical company.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.890
You're not doing so much of this

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.410
actually during the heat
of the battle itself.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.780
It's usually once the
initial firefight gets done.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:19.700
There's four categories when we triage.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:21.100
So he went through all four of them.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.350
Dead, expectant, they need your help,

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:24.360
and then they're OK.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.090
The guy's hollering and yelling.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:27.350
If they're yelling, they're OK.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:28.820
So it's a routine.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:32.117
And then he had one guy that's a priority.

00:05:32.117 --> 00:05:33.650
And then that guy was already dead.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:34.930
Confirming someone that's dead

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.860
in a few seconds is not easy to do.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:40.130
I was in Ramadi when we had 200 people

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.310
that were bombed at
one time from one bomb.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.490
We got 70 of these casualties
that came right to us.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.840
Just coming in, and they
all come in one by one.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.370
And I was the triage officer.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:51.580
The first Marine I got was expectant,

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.810
but I couldn't tell for sure

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:57.440
because I only had
literally four, five seconds

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.310
to decide whether he was dead or not.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:02.200
And there was no way we
could use our resources

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.958
to work on them. So what you're doing

00:06:03.958 --> 00:06:06.971
is trying to cherry-pick the easy saves.

00:06:06.971 --> 00:06:08.820
So you don't want to
spend too much time on it,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.060
which is very different than
what we do in civilian trauma.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.568
In civilian trauma, we go full bore,

00:06:14.568 --> 00:06:17.293
hardcore team of 12 on top of one person

00:06:17.293 --> 00:06:18.370
for hours and hours.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.680
And that's not something
you can do in the military.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.723
I'd give it a 9.

00:06:22.862 --> 00:06:23.695
Jesus!

00:06:23.695 --> 00:06:24.528
Bring it in! Bring it in!

00:06:24.528 --> 00:06:26.143
I said get his guts in now!

00:06:28.252 --> 00:06:30.380
What they're trying to
show is an evisceration.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:33.010
So, a guy got hit,
they're taking enemy fire,

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:36.660
high-velocity weapons, AK-47s
out there, getting hit.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.540
And then you eviscerate your organs.

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:40.160
They're making an attempt
to push it back in.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:42.885
You can't do it. It doesn't work that way.

00:06:42.885 --> 00:06:46.950
The ones I saw were from
exit wounds of AK-47s

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:48.970
through a little small plum-size hole.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:51.470
You will shove everything
out because you got pressure

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.650
as you're grunting and
moaning from the pain.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:55.290
And then you can't shove it back in

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:56.710
because it just pops right back out.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.220
So unless they're chemically
paralyzed by intubating them,

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.390
it's a waste of time.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:04.020
In penetrating injury,

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.970
when we're talking about bullet
wounds and so on like that,

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:10.570
the main thing is just get
them to me as soon as you can

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:12.040
and stop the bleeding.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:12.950
If it's not your heart

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:14.610
and your major vessels, we can save you.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.771
If you don't hit those,
the rest we can repair.

00:07:16.771 --> 00:07:19.253
Hey! Hey! Look at me!

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:22.350
Now, the one thing I
hate when they do movies

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.730
is every time you get some
sort of torso injuries

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:27.280
they make them spit blood.
And that doesn't happen.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.190
Unless you get shot in the mouth

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:30.760
or you have something
going through your cheeks

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.140
and your throat, up high.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:33.040
You don't vomit blood

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.238
from getting a gut shot
or anything like that.

00:07:35.238 --> 00:07:36.671
Ready, up!

00:07:37.529 --> 00:07:38.421
Let's move!

00:07:38.421 --> 00:07:40.205
The one thing I do like is they do show

00:07:40.205 --> 00:07:42.610
how difficult it is to move
a heavy body out there.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:46.900
These are 200- to 300-pound
masses that you got to drag.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:50.020
And two guys pulling these
things is not that easy to do.

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.262
I think that part's well done.

00:07:55.488 --> 00:07:58.485
You'd never drop in a bird
when there's fire going on.

00:07:58.485 --> 00:07:59.676
And a bird's not going to come down

00:07:59.676 --> 00:08:02.626
to go do a casualty evacuation

00:08:02.626 --> 00:08:04.310
when they're going to be shooting

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:06.263
at a $40 million bird up in the air.

00:08:06.263 --> 00:08:08.850
You'll only land when it's a
really secure landing zone.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:10.740
I'd give it about a 6.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:12.210
Hawkeye: You operate on a human time bomb

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:13.407
while your life flashes in front of you

00:08:13.407 --> 00:08:14.990
and you promise God anything he wants

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.710
if it will keep your patient
from blowing up in your face.

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.730
He's doing a spinal anesthesia there.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:22.540
I like how he put the needle
in and he's injecting.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:24.450
They did this in the Korean War.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.517
Back then they used a lot
of regional anesthesia.

00:08:31.763 --> 00:08:33.490
OK. Get it out of here.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:35.690
So he obviously got shot

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.880
in the buttock region, pelvis region,

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:39.540
and he's got a live ordnance in there.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:41.693
So this is a real scenario. This happens.

00:08:41.693 --> 00:08:44.720
I think that's a rifle
grenade that they shoot.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.610
And usually that would not go into him,

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:48.470
because this doesn't have a sharp point

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.303
or anything like that.

00:08:49.303 --> 00:08:53.991
But rocket-propelled,
RPGs, as they call it,

00:08:53.991 --> 00:08:56.640
we did actually have
that during Freedom War.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.850
There's a complete protocol about this.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.380
You usually take them far
away from the hospital

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.000
in an area that's fortified.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.700
You take one volunteer.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.050
You say, "Who volunteers
to get this medal?"

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:09.502
Because if you come out of
it alive, you get a medal.

00:09:09.502 --> 00:09:10.720
And then you try to minimize

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:12.260
the number of people that go out there.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.737
The chances of it going off

00:09:13.737 --> 00:09:16.620
if it hasn't gone off
at that point are small,

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:18.650
but you want to minimize the risks

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:19.890
of doing things like that.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.650
There was a story, I think, in,

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:25.326
it was 2006 in Afghanistan, Maj. John Oh,

00:09:25.326 --> 00:09:27.125
a West Point grad doctor

00:09:27.125 --> 00:09:30.750
who had a guy got a RPG in his pelvis.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:32.670
I think the grenade portion wasn't there,

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.950
but the tail end of it
was sticking out of him.

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:37.620
They took a six-man team
and extracted it out.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:38.610
So that does happen.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.160
My experience in the military,

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.370
in the war, was exactly like "M*A*S*H."

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.270
The mud, the dirt, the people,

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:46.250
the stories were exactly like "M*A*S*H."

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.340
And it was just a ton of fun

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:49.880
being out there helping out the troops.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.090
You get to help out people
who were injured out there.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.330
So that's the fun part.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:54.240
The clothes that they wear,

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:56.243
all that stuff is pretty realistic.

00:09:56.243 --> 00:09:58.023
I give this one a 9 1/2.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:07.800
Yeah, I hate this part.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:09.240
If a guy at that close range

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.440
shoots you with an AK-47 with a 7.6 round,

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:13.820
that's going to destroy you.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:15.510
But, I mean, it was really peripheral.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:18.490
So it somehow just got
the most minor of wounds.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:20.120
But then they show that picture

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.630
of the very disproportion
of the right lower lobe

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:23.760
with a little air.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.000
And what they're trying to show is

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:26.890
this thing called a tension pneumothorax.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:30.380
The lung collapses to
a little small thing.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.350
And then if the air continues to do it,

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:34.040
then you get so much pressure

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:36.430
that it pushes on the heart
and the heart doesn't fill.

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:37.620
Then your blood pressure falls,

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:38.570
and you could die from that.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:40.450
That's what they're trying to mimic here.

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:41.933
And this is really rare.

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:48.070
Usually if you have a hole
in a lung, it bubbles out.

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:49.690
So it's usually always full of blood

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:50.760
and blood is coming out.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:58.430
In a situation like that,

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:00.890
you would have no breath
sounds on one side,

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:02.800
and you would have breath
sounds on the other side.

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.470
But we know that even with
stethoscopes when we do this,

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:07.730
it's very misleading.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:09.410
Because when you put it on the side

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:10.350
that does have the tension,

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.700
you can still hear the
other side of the lung.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.343
So we have learned that that
physical exam is not very good.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:24.840
At that time during that war,

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:26.550
they would not have this Veress needle,

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:29.150
that special needle that he
were using in this scene.

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:31.260
There's a lot of problems
with that approach.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:32.940
It is a sharp needle.

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:35.530
It can cause a lot of injury to the lungs.

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:37.120
And the way he turns it,

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:38.400
he doesn't really relieve it.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.933
And it wasn't the greatest.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:47.350
He had a three-way stopcock.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:49.800
So that's a way that has a valve.

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.100
It's not an automatic valve.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:52.930
It's something that you
have to open and close

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:54.380
in one direction or another.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:57.020
To sit around with this
hardcore needle in your chest,

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.408
and you're walking around completely awake

00:11:59.408 --> 00:12:02.190
and alert and doing tension
on it every few minutes,

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:03.590
it's way far-fetched.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:04.931
From a medical perspective,

00:12:04.931 --> 00:12:07.900
the way they did the wounds
and everything like that,

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:09.352
I'd give it a 2 1/2.

00:12:09.352 --> 00:12:10.443
Stay with me.

00:12:10.443 --> 00:12:12.194
Talk to me, guys, talk
to me. What happened?

00:12:12.194 --> 00:12:13.740
Oh, s---, is that Kirk?

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.100
There's some realism in there.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.200
This is an outpost.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:19.590
This looks like Army.

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:21.380
So Army Italian base station.

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:22.730
Army will usually have a medic,

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:24.650
or they'll have a physician's assistant.

00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:26.970
Usually with as much webbing and stuff,

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:28.093
the unit that they're in,

00:12:28.093 --> 00:12:30.120
the housing unit they're
in, is just paper thin.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:31.770
So the bullets would
go right through there.

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.913
They should be a little bit more fortified

00:12:33.913 --> 00:12:36.780
with the Hesco barriers of dirt.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.650
You notice that the guy's bagging a guy

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:39.960
'cause he's not breathing well,

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:41.290
and they're about to do CPR.

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:42.340
We don't do that out there.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.440
If they need CPR, that's wasting time,

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:45.930
because they need blood.

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:48.080
Medevac won't be here for a few hours,

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:50.650
which means he needs fresh
blood if he wants to live.

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:51.483
This is the only thing we can do.

00:12:51.483 --> 00:12:53.243
We're not sterile, and we
can't test for disease.

00:12:53.243 --> 00:12:55.280
Our fresh blood is all
we can do to save him.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.920
Gloves again, with the bloody hands.

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:58.410
That's not really realistic.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:12:59.780
They have gloves at this point.

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:00.613
When I went out there,

00:13:00.613 --> 00:13:01.830
we started doing this for the first time.

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:05.460
We actually started to really
use warm, fresh whole blood,

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:07.260
which is miraculous.

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:08.820
It's unbelievably miraculous.

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:10.820
We can't do that in the civilian world,

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:12.560
but we did walking blood bank.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:17.070
In my unit in the Ramadi
Charlie Med, two-surgeon shop,

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:20.940
we had about 60, 70
medics with us as well.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.520
The base was about 600 people.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.390
So we'd go to the chow hall,

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:27.060
and there would be a guy that screams out,

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:28.550
"Walking blood bank."

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:30.690
Everybody would spit out their food,

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:33.032
go line up at my medical company,

00:13:33.032 --> 00:13:34.429
check their dog tags.

00:13:34.429 --> 00:13:35.870
They lay down on the floor.

00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:39.028
They would poke them, get a bag of blood.

00:13:39.028 --> 00:13:40.490
We would do a little test to see

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:42.140
if they were compatible when we used it.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:44.430
So from that perspective, it's true.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:46.210
There's a lot of little
mistakes that they made

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:48.494
through here in order to dramatize it,

00:13:48.494 --> 00:13:50.810
and they can get away
with it for most people.

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:53.798
But you wouldn't be walking up to them

00:13:53.798 --> 00:13:56.330
and giving them your blood
standing up and things like that.

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:57.460
At least they're wearing
the right uniform.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.040
So I give them 7.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:00.315
Take the plasma.

00:14:03.473 --> 00:14:06.930
Hey! Hey, give us a hand!

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:08.570
He happens to have miraculously

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:13.070
one bottle of plasma and rubber tubings.

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:13.903
That's what they use.

00:14:13.903 --> 00:14:15.600
Bottle of plasma. It wasn't clear,

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:16.730
it was usually a little yellow.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:17.880
You take your blood,

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.550
and then if you put it
into a machine and spin it,

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:21.880
all the red cells are heavy.

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:23.230
They go out to the outside,

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:26.520
and that's your, what we call,
your packed red blood cells;

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:27.923
a thin layer of white cells;

00:14:27.923 --> 00:14:29.895
and the rest of the water,

00:14:29.895 --> 00:14:32.140
which has proteins in
it, is called plasma.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:35.960
And it has all these proteins
in it called factors,

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:37.870
which help stop bleeding as well.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.270
And it's a very, very
good resuscitative tool,

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:43.980
much, much better than
using the bags of IV,

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:46.038
the saline and the lactated Ringer's,

00:14:46.038 --> 00:14:47.304
that we used in Vietnam.

00:14:47.304 --> 00:14:49.650
I like the idea that they
brought the plasma into it.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:51.228
I'd give this a 6, 7.

00:14:51.228 --> 00:14:54.320
Medic: BP 80 over 60,
pulse 112, severe burns,

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.472
dyspnea, signs of shock.

00:14:56.472 --> 00:14:59.779
We got to take care of enemy combatants.

00:14:59.779 --> 00:15:03.000
We'd get soldier A and the
guy who shot soldier A.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.530
We'd get to take care of both of them.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:06.250
We'd get to take care of civilians.

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:07.490
I took care of 6-year-olds

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:11.500
with big hunk of metal
sticking out of her neck.

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:13.130
Fortunately, missed her spinal cord.

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:14.900
Burn wounds from explosions are common.

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:16.717
When you get civilians, they get burned.

00:15:16.717 --> 00:15:18.609
Military people we
didn't see a lot of burns

00:15:18.609 --> 00:15:20.360
because, again, you're
always in the vehicles.

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:22.190
The setting is pretty realistic

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:23.700
for depending on where you are.

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.970
You know, they come in
by stretchers like that.

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:28.590
Field hospital, they have, you know,

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:32.040
lots and lots of doctors
and areas of triage

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:35.152
and care versus a very small area forward.

00:15:35.152 --> 00:15:36.248
Two units Ringer's in the field,

00:15:36.248 --> 00:15:38.050
5 milligrams morphine administered.

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:38.900
Where is she burned?

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:41.736
Total body surface burned,

00:15:41.736 --> 00:15:43.709
I estimate 45%.

00:15:43.709 --> 00:15:46.480
The acting, the shaking, the convulsing.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:47.850
I don't know what that was all about.

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:49.970
He would see a person that's moulaged

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:52.660
with thoracic and frontal extremity burns,

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:55.630
which is quite severe,
probably closer to 80%, 90%.

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:57.900
But then he's asking the
other guy where he's burned.

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:00.060
He's looking at the person,
and the guy says "legs."

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:01.690
Well, it's obvious to the viewer

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:04.190
that's he's burned, you know, everywhere.

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:06.690
The dialogue was not really realistic

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.290
and not fun for me.

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:09.860
And everything was very scripted.

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:11.720
I just didn't enjoy
this part of it at all.

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:12.840
If there was a way to mess it up,

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:14.190
they messed it up for some reason.

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:15.493
So I give it about a 5.

00:16:16.842 --> 00:16:17.750
Just stay with me, Smurf.

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:20.543
Keep talking. Keep making
noises for me, yeah?

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:23.870
I see it.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:25.780
The medical care was good.

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:27.460
It's exactly what she should have done.

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:28.370
I was happy with that.

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:30.279
She packed it. She dressed it.

00:16:30.279 --> 00:16:31.660
She tried a tourniquet.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:33.460
And sometimes they still bleed out.

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:34.733
So that was believable.

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:37.543
Come on, Smurf.

00:16:39.434 --> 00:16:42.132
Normally you want to put
tourniquet time on the face

00:16:42.132 --> 00:16:43.800
and say when you put the tourniquet on.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.030
Usually we'll do black
markers for that, Sharpies.

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:47.720
I want to know whether
that tourniquet's been on

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:49.720
for five minutes or 45 minutes.

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:51.140
If it's been on for two hours,

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:52.968
you're going to have a dead
leg you got to cut off.

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:05.813
Vietnam War, Huey helicopter
from the United States shows up

00:17:05.813 --> 00:17:08.223
and cranks her to the top of that place.

00:17:08.223 --> 00:17:10.200
And her coming up there
with the pressure on it,

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:11.620
that's feasible and doable,

00:17:11.620 --> 00:17:13.743
strategically just didn't
make a lot of sense.

00:17:13.743 --> 00:17:15.060
I'm going to say, because, you know,

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:17.200
the medical part was pretty decent,

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:18.920
I'd give it a 5, 6.

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:20.180
You know what I thought was more accurate?

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:23.462
The top three, I would say,
were "Saving Private Ryan,

00:17:23.462 --> 00:17:25.530
"Hacksaw Ridge," and "M*A*S*H."

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:26.363
Thanks for watching.

00:17:26.363 --> 00:17:29.223
If you enjoyed this video,
click on the link above.

